1
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:04,454
When a neural network learns, it

2
00:00:04,454 --> 00:00:06,715
is mostly doing one simple thing:

3
00:00:06,715 --> 00:00:08,049
walking downhill.

4
00:00:08,049 --> 00:00:10,601
This ball is running gradient descent,

5
00:00:10,601 --> 00:00:12,457
and in 28 steps it reaches

6
00:00:12,457 --> 00:00:14,254
the bottom of the valley.

7
00:00:14,254 --> 00:00:15,878
Let's see how it knows

8
00:00:15,878 --> 00:00:17,270
which way is down.

9
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:18,837
Picture the loss as a

10
00:00:18,837 --> 00:00:20,636
curve over one parameter.

11
00:00:20,636 --> 00:00:22,667
At any point, the slope tells

12
00:00:22,667 --> 00:00:24,408
you which way is uphill.

13
00:00:24,408 --> 00:00:27,367
Gradient descent reduces the loss by stepping

14
00:00:27,367 --> 00:00:30,152
in the direction of the negative gradient.

15
00:00:30,152 --> 00:00:31,777
Steep slope, big step.

16
00:00:31,777 --> 00:00:33,460
Flat slope, small step.

17
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:35,839
How far each step goes

18
00:00:35,839 --> 00:00:38,132
is the learning rate.

19
00:00:38,132 --> 00:00:40,851
At 0.02, the loss is still

20
00:00:40,851 --> 00:00:43,570
about 0.02 after 80 steps.

21
00:00:43,570 --> 00:00:46,033
At 0.08, the loss falls

22
00:00:46,033 --> 00:00:48,837
below 0.001 after 28 steps.

23
00:00:48,837 --> 00:00:52,235
At 0.17, each step overshoots, and

24
00:00:52,235 --> 00:00:55,208
the loss grows to over 8,000.

25
00:00:55,208 --> 00:00:58,182
On this surface, any learning

26
00:00:58,182 --> 00:01:01,410
rate above about 0.167 diverges.

27
00:01:01,410 --> 00:01:03,919
Real models train on huge datasets,

28
00:01:03,919 --> 00:01:06,366
so computing the exact gradient on

29
00:01:06,366 --> 00:01:07,835
all of it is slow.

30
00:01:07,835 --> 00:01:11,567
Stochastic gradient descent estimates the gradient from

31
00:01:11,567 --> 00:01:14,137
a random subset of the data instead.

32
00:01:14,137 --> 00:01:15,973
The path gets noisy, but

33
00:01:15,973 --> 00:01:17,870
each step is far cheaper,

34
00:01:17,870 --> 00:01:19,950
and it still heads downhill.

35
00:01:19,950 --> 00:01:21,414
The idea is old:

36
00:01:21,414 --> 00:01:24,276
Augustin-Louis Cauchy first suggested

37
00:01:24,276 --> 00:01:26,339
gradient descent in 1847.

38
00:01:26,339 --> 00:01:29,267
Measure the slope, step the other way,

39
00:01:29,267 --> 00:01:32,062
choose a sensible step size, repeat.

40
00:01:32,062 --> 00:01:34,059
That's gradient descent.

41
00:01:34,059 --> 00:01:35,856
I'm Compiled, see you

42
00:01:35,856 --> 00:01:37,320
in the next one.
